TI  - A Case Study of Non-Face-to-Face Cognitive Behavior Therapy to Improve the Executive Function of Adult Women with ADHD
TI  - ADHD 성인 여성의 실행기능 증진을 위한 비대면 인지행동치료 사례연구
T2  - Korean Journal of Cognitive Behavior Therapy and Counseling
T2  - 인지행동치료상담연구
M3  - research-article
AB  - The purpose of this study is to examine the effect of Cognitive Behavior Therapy (CBT) on the improvement of executive function in adult women with ADHD. In this case, CBT is structured around cognitive interventions like psychoeducation, self-monitoring, and the cognitive restructuring of maladaptive automatic thoughts and core beliefs. Additionally, the treatment plan incorporates behavioral interventions such as relaxation through breathing exercises, training in time management and organizational skills, communication skills training, and problem-solving strategies. The study participant is a woman in her 40s employed full-time and raising an elementary-aged child. Therapy sessions were administered remotely via video at M Research Institute, occurring weekly from March to September 2022, with each session lasting 50 minutes for a total of 20 sessions. To evaluate the therapy’s effectiveness, subjective feedback from the client was gathered within each session, and cognitive, emotional, and behavioral changes were assessed through detailed session records. The key findings of this study are as follows. First, throughout the sessions, the client became aware of maladaptive thoughts and core beliefs associated with her ADHD symptoms and restructured them into more adaptive patterns. Second, after undergoing CBT, the client perceived reductions in her levels of depression, anxiety, and anger. Third, the therapy led to improvements in her ability to systematically carry out work-related tasks, along with strengthened interpersonal coping skills and enhanced parenting techniques.
